## **1\. Three Positioning Strategies**

Core premise: Only three positioning strategies exist. Every product fits one. The choice is made based on market data, founder instinct, and available resources. None are silver bullets. Each can produce massive success or failure.

### **Strategy 1: Position in a Mature Category**

What it is: Enter an established category and claim you're better than everyone else in that category. Primary goal is market share theft from category leaders.

A category is mature when it has: established leading vendors, clear buying criteria, external validation from analysts like Gartner, dedicated conferences and communities, and a shared vocabulary the market recognizes.

Core Customer Reference Point: The name of the category itself. Competitive Alternatives: Other vendors in the category. Target Customer: People who have or are shopping for the category.

Advantages:

* Mind space and budget already exist. People explicitly shop for it, actively search for your type of solution.  
* No education required. Customers already have it or have seriously evaluated it. The bulk of up-funnel marketing work has already been done.

Risks:

* The more mature a category, the bigger the competitors. Market leaders get the bulk of shelf space and win the majority of deals by default.  
* Won't work for people who don't know the category. You're making a conscious decision to prioritize the category-aware.

Choose when:

* You believe the category is large enough to meet growth goals (measured in actual revenue across all vendors, not theoretical potential)  
* You have resources to go head-to-head with market leaders, OR  
* You can own an underserved segment that is large enough to be meaningful (e.g. DuckDuckGo targeting privacy-minded search users — \~$100M ARR)

Examples: Figma, iPhone, Zoom, Salesforce, ServiceNow, Workday, Snowflake, Walmart

### **Strategy 2: Position in an Immature Category**

What it is: Enter an existing but nascent category and sell it to people who have never heard of the category. You're not inventing it — you're popularizing it to the category-unaware.

Core Customer Reference Point: The job-to-be-done of the category. Competitive Alternatives: The status quo way people currently do the category's JTBD. Target Customer: People doing the JTBD who don't know a category solution exists.

Category existence test: Ask an LLM "what are the top 5 vendors of \[category\]?" — clear ranked list \= category exists; asks for clarification \= probably doesn't yet.

Advantages:

* Greenfield. Targeting people who don't know other vendors exist. Can focus marketing on how you fix their broken way of doing things.  
* Growing categories get VC attention and funding.

Risks:

* You must bring the product to buyers. They won't come looking.  
* You inherit the market's opinion of the category — including any negative press (AI SDRs, NFTs, blockchain).  
* Some categories fizzle as fast as they grow.  
* Enterprise deals require a head-to-head competitive story anyway for late-stage evaluations.

Choose when:

* You believe the category will grow and you can become one of the defining leaders when it solidifies.  
* You have the marketing DNA for a long-game educational content motion — consistently, patiently, over time.

Examples: DocuSign, Dropbox, Google, Uber, Tesla, Slack, 1Password

### **Strategy 3: Position to Create a New Category**

What it is: Refuse any existing category. Define the problem, JTBD, and category from scratch.

Core Customer Reference Point: The job-to-be-done of your choosing. Competitive Alternatives: The status quo way people currently do the JTBD you selected. Target Customer: People doing the JTBD you selected.

Advantages:

* You design the category — including the size and shape of the problem.  
* Distribution head start: if you gain traction, others adopt your language and you're already the reference point.

Risks:

* Picking the right JTBD size is hard — too broad loses credibility ("grow revenue" is not a category), too narrow limits scale.  
* First mover rarely wins. Vrbo before Airbnb. AltaVista before Google. Uber wasn't first in rideshare. Slack had a large head start over Teams — and lost.  
* Market may reject the new category entirely. Docusign's "Intelligent Agreement Management" was laughed out of the room.

Choose when:

* Any existing category label would hurt more than help.  
* You're willing to play a long, expensive, patient game.

Examples: eBay, VMware, Red Bull, Gong, Xerox, Brex, Clay

Cross-strategy principle: The choice is a bet — on market dynamics, your resources, and your ability to execute the specific motion each strategy demands.

