## **3\. Positioning as Differentiator (Same Features, Different GTM)**

Core premise: When competitors have the same features, positioning is the product. The roadmap is a commodity. The GTM strategy — specifically WHO you target and HOW you message — is the differentiator.

### **The Two-Column Framework**

Same across all competitors:

* Product category  
* Features

Where differentiation lives:

* Target ICPs (who you're for)  
* Competitive alternatives (what you're replacing)  
* Problem you name and own  
* Benefits you emphasize

### **Slack / Discord / Microsoft Teams Example**

All three: communication platform with threads, reactions, channels, video chat. Identical product surface.

|  | Target ICP | Alternatives | Problem Named | Benefit Claimed |
| ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |
| Slack | Startups & growing enterprises | Email, Teams, Skype | Clunky, poor UI, boring | Work faster and more flexibly |
| Discord | School clubs, gaming groups, art communities | iMessage, In-Game Chat | High latency, not cross-platform | Easy to talk and hang out more often |
| Microsoft Teams | Everyone (home \+ business) | Email, Slack, Skype, iMessage | Not connected with Office365 accounts | Improve Office365 workflows |

Strategic read: Microsoft leverages existing advantages — installed base, platform lock-in, distribution. That's an incumbent move.

Startups don't have those advantages. Being specific about WHO you target is not a nice-to-have. It's the only path to carving out a defensible position. Trying to target everyone with the same features as the market leader is how you become invisible.

The principle: ICP specificity is a startup's primary competitive weapon. The more precisely you name who you're for — and what problem you're solving for them specifically — the more you can differentiate on positioning alone, even when the product is functionally equivalent to larger competitors.

