## **6\. Anti-Value Prop**

Core premise: Mapping who you DON'T help is as strategically powerful as mapping who you do. Clarity on your anti-persona builds a sharper narrative, creates a passionate following, and drives internal alignment on what you're actually building.

### **The Framework**

Value Prop: We help \[Persona\] struggling with \[Problem\] solved by \[Solution\] so they can \[Benefit\] Anti-Value Prop: We DON'T help \[Anti-Persona\] struggling with \[Anti-Problem\] solved by \[Anti-Solution\] so they can \[Anti-Benefit\]

### **The Four Anti-Value Prop Components**

Anti-Persona: Who is your product NOT for? People who are in your TAM but outside your target segments.

Anti-Problem: What does this anti-persona struggle with? A problem you've consciously decided is NOT worth solving. These "unworthy" problems are often symptoms of a larger problem your product does solve — but attacking them directly would lead you somewhere you don't want to go.

Anti-Solution: How do they solve that unworthy problem? Your take on why that solution is bad — expensive, overly complex, doesn't actually work, solves the wrong thing.

Anti-Benefit: What outcome does the anti-solution create? A value you explicitly DON'T optimize for. This is where you jab at the status quo mindset — naming what the market thinks it wants, and saying: that's not what we're building toward.

### **Basecamp Example**

|  | Value Prop | Anti-Value Prop |
| ----- | ----- | ----- |
| Persona | Startups & small teams | Large enterprise teams |
| Problem | Project tool overload & poor project results | Communication and data "visibility" |
| Solution | Simple PM tool that keeps communication and work in one place | Custom & complex PM platform with 100s of integrations |
| Benefit | Focus on the work and create better projects/products | Show their bosses how much they work |

### **Why This Works**

The anti-value prop does three things simultaneously:

1. Repels bad-fit customers before they waste your sales team's time  
2. Signals to good-fit customers that you built this specifically for them  
3. Forces internal clarity — when you've named what you're not solving, it becomes much harder for the roadmap to drift toward it

Spiky POVs draw the right people in by pushing the wrong people away. The sharper the anti-value prop, the stronger the signal.

