## **12\. Startup Website Messaging Guide**

Core premise: Each page has a specific purpose in your GTM. The first three pages are acquisition — get these wrong and nothing else matters. The rest only become valuable after you've landed the intro message with an ICP.

### **Priority 1: Acquisition Pages (get these right first)**

Home Page — The Front Door: Purpose: Introduce the core aspect of your product and the problem it solves. Lead with: Most compelling capability and use case. Goal: Get the ideal user to take the first step (sign up, book demo, go to sub-page). The bridge: Connect the user's situation to the main capability. They should think "this is built exactly for me." Good example: Calendly — super clear on the product and what the problem is.

Persona Page — The Personal Welcome: Purpose: Highlight who you help and the ways you help them. Lead with: Most compelling capability and use case for that specific ICP. Goal: Get the ideal user to take the first step. One page per persona type. Each gets their own message. Good example: Asana — very clear who the page is for, highlights use cases, directs to sub-pages.

Use Case Page — The VIP Entrance: Purpose: Highlight how a specific user will use the product. Lead with: The main capability that addresses the use case and how it works. Goal: Get the ideal user to take the first step. These are the highest-converting pages when done right because specificity \= relevance. Good example: Zapier — thousands of use case pages, each so specific they convert extremely well.

### **Priority 2: Secondary Pages (valuable after landing the intro message)**

Product Page — The Feature Index: Showcase main features and how they work. Get users to see all the cool things they can do. Good example: Slack — navigational dropdown, each feature gets its own page.

Pricing Page — The Value Exchange: Layout the value exchange for users and buyers. Clear description of what type of user fits each pricing tier. Good example: Airtable — clean, personalized by user type, makes self-selection easy.

About Page — The Big Vision: Express beliefs and big vision. Usually pulled from investor deck. Good example: Canva — clear mission, highlights key stats and ambitions.

Resource Pages — The Owner's Manual: Deep-dive guides to get users to value faster. Most fundamental onboarding guides. Good example: Notion — enormous volume of valuable information, gold mine for current users.

### **The Priority Split**

Get Home Page, Persona Pages, and Use Case Pages right first. These are the entrance paths — they connect the prospect's situation to your product's capability.

The conversation with a prospect has already started before they hit your site — via outbound email, ad, or content. The mindset they arrive with must be factored into how these pages are written.

GTM model dependency: How you use these pages depends entirely on your GTM motion. Don't copy another company's page structure without understanding how their acquisition motion differs from yours.

