## **15\. 2026 Customer Intel — Top 4 Positioning Challenges**

Source: 51 sales calls with B2B founders conducted in the last 60 days (Fletch research, 2026).

Challenge 1: AI bleeding into every category "How do we differentiate when everyone sounds the same with AI?" Every competitor has bolted AI onto their messaging. The category language has homogenized. Differentiation on AI capability alone is no longer possible because the claim is universal.

Challenge 2: Product outpacing the marketing "We've evolved a lot. Our website doesn't reflect what we do today. How do we update our messaging without losing what worked?" Product has moved — new capabilities, new ICP — but website still describes what the company was 18 months ago. The risk cuts both ways: update too aggressively and lose the positioning that drove current traction; don't update and become invisible to buyers you can now actually serve.

Challenge 3: Multiple market creep "We serve multiple ICPs. Do we pick one or try to speak to all of them?" Started focused, got traction in one segment, then took deals from adjacent segments because revenue is revenue. Now the ICP is blurry, the messaging is trying to serve everyone, and nothing resonates sharply.

Challenge 4: Multiple product creep "We have multiple products. Do we position the platform or each piece?" A product suite has emerged and the company is deciding whether to lead with the platform story or let each product carry its own positioning. Platform positioning risks abstraction. Product-level positioning risks fragmentation. The answer depends on where buyers enter and how they buy.

The pattern underneath all four: Every challenge is a version of the same problem — the positioning was set at one stage and the company has since outgrown it.

