## **19\. Two Core Positioning Beliefs (Stand-Out vs. Education Game)**

Core premise: Every positioning strategy comes down to one of two beliefs. Each belief pulls you into a completely different go-to-market strategy.

### **Belief 1: Stand-Out Game**

"We can stand out in an existing category." Buyers already know they have a problem. They're evaluating vendors in the category. Your job: win the comparison — be the obvious choice when someone is already in motion.

What this requires:

* Win high-intent search terms  
* Build sharp comparison content ("why us vs. them" pages)  
* Stack proof that speaks directly to the buyer's existing evaluation criteria  
* Case studies, reviews, G2/Capterra, "why us vs. them" pages  
* Every asset designed to win the moment a buyer is already looking

You're competing for attention that already exists. The demand is there. The question is whether you show up compellingly when buyers go looking.

### **Belief 2: Distribution and Education Game**

"We can galvanize a bunch of people to come to our new category." Buyers may know they have the problem but aren't aware a solution like yours exists — so they're not searching for it. Your job isn't just to win the comparison. It's to make the comparison happen.

What this requires:

* Meet buyers in the context of the problem, not the product  
* Thought leadership, community, partnerships  
* Content that speaks to the pain before it speaks to the solution  
* Earn trust and build urgency upstream — when buyers eventually start looking, you're already the frame of reference they use to evaluate everyone else

You're not losing deals because your pitch is unclear. You're losing because you're not even getting the opportunity to pitch. Buyers aren't searching for you — they don't know to.

### **The Critical Distinction**

Stand-out game: capture existing demand. Distribution and education game: create the demand first.

They require different content, different channels, different timelines, different metrics, and different budget allocations.

A company that believes it's playing the stand-out game but invests in education content will wonder why nothing converts. A company that believes it's playing the education game but only invests in comparison pages will wonder why awareness never builds.

Know which game you're in. Build the motion that matches it.

