## **20\. Positioning Credibility (Levels of Momentum)**

Core premise: Your positioning strategy is only as strong as your ability to back it up. A valid strategy with insufficient credibility doesn't just underperform — it actively damages trust when the gap becomes visible.

### **The Credibility Gap Problem**

Same words. Same strategy. Wildly different market reception.

Salesforce: "Build, deploy, and manage AI agents at scale" → Market reaction: Innovative. We need this. Let's get a demo.

Your startup: "Build, deploy, and manage AI agents at scale" → Market reaction: More AI noise. No way they can pull this off. Pass.

This isn't a messaging problem. It's a credibility problem. Salesforce has 25+ years of enterprise software, 83,000 employees, $5.5B in R\&D, and 10,000 paying Agentforce customers behind the claim. The startup has a great idea, $15M in the bank, 29 customers, and most revenue from professional services.

The strategy is valid. The credibility isn't there yet.

### **The Deal Killer Pattern**

Positioning earns the meeting. Lack of credibility kills the deal.

Bold positioning gets you in the door. But when a prospect asks "how many times have you done this before?" — the gap surfaces. Three times, once for their specific use case, is not enough to justify the risk. The meeting ends — not because the product is bad, but because the proof doesn't support the claim.

### **The Levels of Momentum Framework (Fletch's own progression)**

Level 1 — Own something narrow enough to dominate completely: "We'll audit your homepage and show you why it's working — or not." → A couple hundred of those. Proof accumulates.

Level 2 — Credibility earned the right to claim something bigger: "We'll rewrite your homepage so it's actually clear." → A couple hundred of those. Case studies form. People started tagging Fletch in positioning conversations they weren't part of.

Level 3 — Reputation built the right to say: "We'll fix your positioning strategy." → 500+ engagements later. The claim has teeth.

Each level was only possible because the previous level was actually dominated — not just attempted.

### **The Credibility Question**

Before finalizing positioning, ask one honest question: "What credibility do I actually have right now that makes this claim defensible?"

Not what you're building toward. Not what you'll be able to say in two years. What exists today.

If the answer is thin — the claim is too ambitious. Narrow it to something small enough to build a real reputation around it. Once that reputation exists, level up.

Claiming a position you can't back up doesn't just fail to convert. It trains the market to distrust you before you've earned the right to make the bigger claim.

